<article class="env-remark" id="06HE">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/06HE">Remark <span data-tag="06HE">90.7.11</span>.</a> Let $G : \widehat{\mathcal{C}}_\Lambda \to \textit{Sets}$ be a functor that commutes with limits. Then the map $G \to \widehat{G|_{\mathcal{C}_\Lambda }}$ described in Remark <a href="/tag/06HA" data-tag="06HA">90.7.9</a> is an isomorphism. Indeed, if $S$ is an object of $\widehat{\mathcal{C}}_\Lambda $, then we have canonical bijections </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  \widehat{G|_{\mathcal{C}_\Lambda }}(S) = \mathop{\mathrm{lim}}\nolimits _ n G(S/\mathfrak {m}_ S^ n) = G(\mathop{\mathrm{lim}}\nolimits _ n S/\mathfrak {m}_ S^ n) = G(S).  \]
</div>
  <p> In particular, if $R$ is an object of $\widehat{\mathcal{C}}_\Lambda $ then $\underline{R} = \widehat{\underline{R}|_{\mathcal{C}_\Lambda }}$ because the representable functor $\underline{R}$ commutes with limits by definition of limits. </p>
</article>